ExportPS2Functions.java: export contiguous function ranges

What it fixes: in Ghidra the body of a function can be made of several separate address
ranges. The script wrote one row per function with End = highest address of the body and
Size = number of bytes of the body, so a small routine that jumps to a shared return far away
got a span of tens of KB (12 functions of 24 to 92 bytes had spans of 19 to 118 KB in the
Battle Stadium D.O.N executable), spans overlapped (1,775 nested functions) and Size
differed from End - Start in 1,114 rows.

Rule: let N be the lowest entry point of another function above this function's entry
point (infinity if none).
- The function row goes from the entry point to the end of the last range of the body that
  starts before N (End exclusive, Size = End - Start). Small holes inside the function stay
  inside its row and the row never reaches the next function.
- Every range of the body that starts at or after N, and every range completely before the
  entry point, becomes a label row entry_<8 hex digits> through collectExecutableLabelRecords,
  unless a row already starts at that address.
- A label that is in no function row but lies in the body of a function ends where that
  range of the body ends (never past the next row start). Other labels keep the original
  computation (function row whose span contains them, then the next row start).
- The [ghidra_export] table gets split_function_count (functions with at least one such
  far range or range before the entry point) and body_range_label_count (label rows added).

Rejected first version: "the row covers the range of the body that contains the entry
point". About 1,067 functions have small holes in their body (a few words Ghidra's flow does
not reach); that rule cut each one at its first hole (FUN_00190580 went from 22 KB to 556
bytes) and left the rest to labels that no function row contains (labels outside every
function went from 2,826 to 15,478).

Verification: run tools/funcmap/ghidra_export.sh on SLPS_256.75 and then layout.py,
find_code_pointers.py and validate.py (see docs/tasks/F0-foundations.md, T-025).

Original work for this project, written against PS2Recomp commit
2c5fbb9389e11dd95693385969490c9e8e6f57b4 (same license as that repository).

